  "outputType": "workflow",
  "purpose": "When and how to perform an escharotomy for a circumferential full-thickness burn causing vascular compromise (limb) or restrictive ventilation (chest wall) — a clinical diagnosis and a bedside procedure, distinct from a fasciotomy.",
  "entryPrompt": "This is a clinical diagnosis based on exam findings trending the wrong way, not a fixed time-after-burn trigger — reassess frequently as edema evolves over the first 24-48 hours.",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "recognition",
      "type": "step",
      "prompt": "1. Recognize the indication",
      "body": "LIMB: a CIRCUMFERENTIAL full-thickness (or deep partial-thickness) burn of a LIMB causing vascular compromise:\n- diminished or absent distal pulses (by Doppler if not palpable)\n- progressively worsening capillary refill\n- increasing pain out of proportion or new paresthesia/paralysis\n- a tense, non-compliant limb\n\nThe inelastic eschar acts like a tourniquet as underlying tissue edema builds over the first 24-48 hours post-burn, so this can develop or worsen well after the initial burn assessment, not just immediately.\n\nCHEST: a circumferential CHEST WALL burn causing restrictive ventilation — rising peak airway pressures in a ventilated patient, or worsening respiratory distress/declining tidal volumes in a spontaneously breathing patient, from the eschar mechanically restricting chest wall excursion."
    },
    {
      "id": "limb-technique",
      "type": "step",
      "prompt": "2. Limb escharotomy — technique",
      "body": "WHERE: incise along the MID-AXIAL line (the line connecting the midpoints of the flexion creases, roughly the mid-lateral and/or mid-medial aspect of the limb) — this avoids major neurovascular bundles, which typically run more anteriorly/medially.\n\nHOW DEEP: incise through the ESCHAR ONLY, into the underlying subcutaneous fat — you should feel/see the tissue relax and separate (gaping open) once through the constricting eschar.\n- This is deliberately NOT a fasciotomy (which goes through fascia into muscle compartments for a different indication — traumatic/ischemic compartment syndrome, not burn eschar constriction).\n- Going deeper than needed risks unnecessary injury to underlying structures.\n\nHOW LONG: extend the incision the full length of the circumferential burn, and across involved joints if the burn crosses them.\n\nFor digits, individual digital escharotomies (mid-lateral) may be needed if circumferentially burned and compromised.",
      "diagram": {
        "title": "Escharotomy incision map",
        "caption": "Mid-medial and mid-lateral lines, the full length of the burn and across every joint, ending 1-2 cm into unburned skin; the chest shield joins the anterior axillary lines at the clavicle and the costal margin. Two nerves move the line: at the elbow the medial incision passes anterior to the medial epicondyle to spare the ulnar nerve, and at the knee the lateral incision stays clear of the fibular neck to spare the common peroneal nerve. Stop at the fat — cutting fascia is a fasciotomy, a different procedure.",
