    {
      "id": "chest-technique",
      "type": "step",
      "prompt": "3. Chest wall escharotomy — technique",
      "body": "Bilateral incisions along the anterior axillary lines (or mid-axillary), extended and connected by transverse incisions:\n- at the level of the costal margin inferiorly\n- if needed, near the clavicles superiorly\n\nThis creates a rectangular/grid pattern that releases the constricting eschar across the chest wall to restore adequate excursion.\n\nSame principle: through eschar into subcutaneous tissue, not into deeper structures."
    },
    {
      "id": "reassess",
      "type": "step",
      "prompt": "4. Reassess and manage bleeding",
      "body": "Reassess pulses/capillary refill (limb) or ventilation parameters (chest) immediately after the incision — improvement should be apparent quickly if the escharotomy adequately released the constriction.\n\nEscharotomy through full-thickness burned tissue is typically not very painful (the nerve endings in that tissue are usually already destroyed by the burn itself) and bleeding is often modest since the eschar itself is relatively avascular.\n\nBut bleeding from the incision edges (viable tissue at the wound margin) can still occur and should be controlled with direct pressure/electrocautery as needed."
    }
  ],
  "checklist": [
    "Indication confirmed by clinical exam trend (pulses, capillary refill, pain/sensation for limbs; ventilation parameters for chest) — not assumed from burn size/circumferential appearance alone",
    "Incision along the mid-axial line for limbs, avoiding named neurovascular structures",
    "Depth limited to eschar + subcutaneous fat — NOT into muscle/fascia (that would be a fasciotomy, a different procedure for a different indication)",
    "Full length of the circumferential burn released, across joints if involved",
    "Reassessment performed immediately after to confirm the release was adequate"
  ],
  "crossLinks": [
    "pediatric-burn-management",
    "parkland-formula"
  ],
  "buildNote": "workflow outputType. Explicitly distinguishes escharotomy (through eschar/fat, for burn-constriction) from fasciotomy (through fascia into muscle, for traumatic/ischemic compartment syndrome) since conflating the two is a real and consequential error — going too deep on an escharotomy risks unnecessary injury, while a true traumatic compartment syndrome needs the deeper fasciotomy this procedure does NOT describe."
}
